Sure! Below is a quick example of how you would use the `vOe` function to update the `version` property of an object called `packageInfo`.  
The function will **only** change the value if:

* the property doesn’t exist yet, **or**
* the new value (`"1.2.3"`) is different from the current value.

```javascript
// Assume this is your current packageInfo object (as a JSON string)
const currentPackageInfo = '{"name":"my-app","description":"A cool app","version":"1.0.0"}';

// Call the function
const updatedPackageInfo = functions.vOe({
  r: currentPackageInfo,   // the object to update (stringified)
  e: "version",            // the property name
  t: "1.2.3"               // the new value to assign
});

console.log(updatedPackageInfo);
// Output will be:
// {"name":"my-app","description":"A cool app","version":"1.2.3"}
```

If the `version` property already had `"1.2.3"` as its value, the function would simply return the original object unchanged. If the property didn’t exist at all, it would add it with the new value.
